1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,879
In twenty twenty one, more than a million deaths

2
00:00:02,729 --> 00:00:06,556
worldwide were caused directly by bacteria that antibiotics could

3
00:00:06,406 --> 00:00:07,447
no longer kill.

4
00:00:07,647 --> 00:00:10,853
Resistant infections were linked to about four point seven

5
00:00:10,703 --> 00:00:12,200
million deaths that year.

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,482
The medicines that changed modern medicine are slowly losing

7
00:00:15,332 --> 00:00:16,107
their power.

8
00:00:16,307 --> 00:00:18,678
This is the story of how it happened, and

9
00:00:18,528 --> 00:00:19,631
what still works.

10
00:00:20,481 --> 00:00:22,469
When antibiotics stop working.

11
00:00:23,319 --> 00:00:25,866
Chapter one. The mould that changed medicine.

12
00:00:26,716 --> 00:00:30,265
In nineteen twenty eight, Alexander Fleming noticed something strange

13
00:00:30,115 --> 00:00:31,237
on a culture plate.

14
00:00:31,437 --> 00:00:34,184
A mould had landed on it, and the bacteria

15
00:00:34,034 --> 00:00:35,392
around it had died.

16
00:00:35,592 --> 00:00:38,953
That mould gave us penicillin, the first antibiotic to

17
00:00:38,803 --> 00:00:39,945
change medicine.

18
00:00:40,145 --> 00:00:43,584
Before antibiotics, a simple infection could be a death

19
00:00:43,434 --> 00:00:47,283
sentence. Afterwards, medicine could take risks it never could

20
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:47,753
before.

21
00:00:47,953 --> 00:00:52,135
Today, joint replacements, organ transplants and cancer therapy all

22
00:00:51,985 --> 00:00:54,625
depend on being able to treat infections.

23
00:00:54,825 --> 00:00:58,521
Resistance raises the risk of complications from procedures like

24
00:00:58,371 --> 00:00:59,012
surgery.

25
00:00:59,862 --> 00:01:02,556
Chapter two. How bacteria fight back.

26
00:01:03,406 --> 00:01:06,587
Resistance happens when germs develop the ability to defeat

27
00:01:06,437 --> 00:01:08,254
the drugs designed to kill them.

28
00:01:08,454 --> 00:01:11,800
Every time an antibiotic is used, it puts pressure

29
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:13,179
on bacteria to adapt.

30
00:01:13,379 --> 00:01:16,407
Picture billions of bacteria in an infection. A few

31
00:01:16,257 --> 00:01:19,396
carry a random genetic change that helps them survive

32
00:01:19,246 --> 00:01:19,950
the drug.

33
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:23,591
The antibiotic kills the rest. The survivors now have

34
00:01:23,441 --> 00:01:24,688
room to multiply.

35
00:01:24,888 --> 00:01:28,393
That's evolution, happening in days instead of millions of

36
00:01:28,243 --> 00:01:28,790
years.

37
00:01:28,990 --> 00:01:30,491
They have four main tricks.

38
00:01:30,691 --> 00:01:33,732
Some make enzymes that break the antibiotic down.

39
00:01:33,932 --> 00:01:36,622
Some pump it straight back out, or change the

40
00:01:36,472 --> 00:01:38,556
target so the drug no longer fits.

41
00:01:38,756 --> 00:01:41,089
And some close the doors it uses to get

42
00:01:40,939 --> 00:01:41,308
in.

43
00:01:41,508 --> 00:01:44,857
Worse, bacteria can share resistance with other bacteria that

44
00:01:44,707 --> 00:01:46,457
have never even seen the drug.

45
00:01:46,657 --> 00:01:49,307
They pass it on through mobile pieces of DNA,

46
00:01:49,157 --> 00:01:51,698
which is why resistance can spread so fast.

47
00:01:51,898 --> 00:01:56,138
Medical students, this is classic exam material. Enzymes, efflux

48
00:01:55,988 --> 00:01:58,717
pumps, target changes and reduced entry.

49
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:01,930
Chapter three. The scale of the problem.

50
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:06,059
In twenty twenty three, about one in six lab-confirmed

51
00:02:05,909 --> 00:02:09,586
bacterial infections worldwide were resistant to antibiotics.

52
00:02:09,786 --> 00:02:13,264
Between twenty eighteen and twenty twenty three, resistance rose

53
00:02:13,114 --> 00:02:15,415
in over forty percent of the drug and bug

54
00:02:15,265 --> 00:02:16,337
pairs WHO tracks.

55
00:02:16,537 --> 00:02:19,915
Two gut bacteria keep topping these lists: E. coli

56
00:02:19,765 --> 00:02:20,927
and Klebsiella.

57
00:02:21,127 --> 00:02:24,021
Globally, more than forty percent of E. coli and

58
00:02:23,871 --> 00:02:27,492
over fifty five percent of Klebsiella resist the first-choice

59
00:02:27,342 --> 00:02:29,396
antibiotics for these infections.

60
00:02:29,596 --> 00:02:34,123
Even powerful drugs like carbapenems are losing effectiveness against

61
00:02:33,973 --> 00:02:35,999
Klebsiella and Acinetobacter.

62
00:02:36,199 --> 00:02:38,680
And M R S A, a resistant staph, caused

63
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:41,549
over a hundred thousand deaths in a single year

64
00:02:41,399 --> 00:02:42,267
on its own.

65
00:02:42,467 --> 00:02:45,788
Older people are hit hardest. Since nineteen ninety, resistance

66
00:02:45,638 --> 00:02:48,216
deaths in people over seventy have risen by more

67
00:02:48,066 --> 00:02:49,256
than eighty percent.

68
00:02:50,106 --> 00:02:52,924
Chapter four. Why it matters for South Asia.

69
00:02:53,774 --> 00:02:57,125
WHO estimates resistance is highest in its South-East Asia

70
00:02:56,975 --> 00:03:00,544
and Eastern Mediterranean regions, where one in three reported

71
00:03:00,394 --> 00:03:02,009
infections were resistant.

72
00:03:02,209 --> 00:03:04,870
And forecasts in The Lancet put South Asia among

73
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:07,791
the regions with the highest death rates from resistance

74
00:03:07,641 --> 00:03:08,662
by twenty fifty.

75
00:03:08,862 --> 00:03:12,339
Resistant germs spread in hospitals, in the community, through

76
00:03:12,189 --> 00:03:15,401
food and the environment, and between people and animals.

77
00:03:16,251 --> 00:03:18,587
Chapter five. What actually works.

78
00:03:19,437 --> 00:03:22,594
First, use antibiotics only when they're needed. Ask your

79
00:03:22,444 --> 00:03:24,461
doctor whether you really need one.

80
00:03:24,661 --> 00:03:28,114
Colds are viral, and trials show antibiotics don't help

81
00:03:27,964 --> 00:03:28,468
them.

82
00:03:28,668 --> 00:03:32,565
Antibiotics also have side effects. In cold trials, adults

83
00:03:32,415 --> 00:03:35,677
given antibiotics had more of them than those on

84
00:03:35,527 --> 00:03:36,249
placebo.

85
00:03:36,449 --> 00:03:39,532
Second, prevent infections. Clean hands are one of the

86
00:03:39,382 --> 00:03:41,398
best ways to stop germs spreading.

87
00:03:41,598 --> 00:03:45,097
Vaccines prevent infections too, including resistant ones.

88
00:03:45,297 --> 00:03:48,673
For clinicians: prescribe only when the benefits outweigh the

89
00:03:48,523 --> 00:03:50,339
risks, and pick the right drug.

90
00:03:50,539 --> 00:03:53,970
So, to recap. Resistance is caused by germs adapting

91
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:58,118
under antibiotic pressure, and it spreads between bacteria, people

92
00:03:57,968 --> 00:03:58,922
and animals.

93
00:03:59,122 --> 00:04:01,850
It already causes over a million deaths a year,

94
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:03,944
and it's highest in regions like ours.

95
00:04:04,144 --> 00:04:07,731
Antimicrobial resistance is a major global health threat. But

96
00:04:07,581 --> 00:04:09,062
it isn't a lost battle.

97
00:04:09,262 --> 00:04:11,871
Every antibiotic we don't need to use keeps the

98
00:04:11,721 --> 00:04:13,357
ones we have working longer.

99
00:04:14,207 --> 00:04:17,311
If this helped, subscribe to Paravion Med. Sources are

100
00:04:17,161 --> 00:04:18,385
in the description.
